    <p class="text-lg text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      We see it every week. A small business owner gets a letter from a law firm. Their website isn't ADA-compliant. The demand is simple: pay $4,000+ or face a federal lawsuit.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      They had no idea. <strong>83% of small businesses hit with ADA lawsuits didn't know digital accessibility was a requirement.</strong> They thought it was about physical ramps and doorways. They were wrong.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">The Numbers Don't Lie</h2>

    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      Federal ADA Title III website accessibility lawsuits hit <strong>3,117 in 2025</strong> — a 27% jump from the year before. Most targets? Small businesses with fewer than 50 employees. Restaurants, HVAC companies, dental offices, plumbers. The businesses that can least afford a $4,000 surprise.
    </p>

    <div style="margin: 2rem 0; padding: 1.25rem; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); border-radius: 10px; border-left: 3px solid #10b981;">
      <p style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; color: #10b981; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">$4,000+</p>
      <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.6); font-size: 0.85rem;">Average settlement per ADA website violation</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">The Widget Trap</h2>

    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      Here's what makes this worse: <strong>26% of businesses that got sued had an accessibility widget installed.</strong> They paid for a band-aid, thought they were protected, and still got hit with a lawsuit. Widgets don't make you compliant. They don't protect you in court. Real compliance requires real changes to your website's code, structure, and content.
    </p>

